.TH MEMALIGN 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
memalign_alloc, memalign_free, memalign_alloc_aligned \- Memory allocation with alignment

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/memalign.h>

.IB void*
.IB memalign_alloc (size_t boundary, size_t size);

.IB void
.IB memalign_free (void* ptr);

.IB void*
.IB memalign_alloc_aligned (size_t size);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBmemalign_alloc\fR, \fBmemalign_free\fR, and \fBmemalign_alloc_aligned\fR functions are used to allocate and free memory with specific alignment requirements.

.SH memalign_alloc
The \fBmemalign_alloc\fR function allocates memory with a specified alignment boundary and size. It takes a boundary and size as parameters. The function allocates memory and ensures that the allocated memory is aligned according to the specified boundary.

The function returns a pointer to the allocated memory, or \fBNULL\fR if memory allocation fails.

.SH memalign_free
The \fBmemalign_free\fR function frees memory that was allocated with \fBmemalign_alloc\fR. It takes a pointer to the allocated memory as a parameter. The function frees the memory.

The function does not return any value.

.SH memalign_alloc_aligned
The \fBmemalign_alloc_aligned\fR function allocates memory with a default alignment boundary. It takes a size as a parameter. The function allocates memory and ensures that the allocated memory is aligned to the default boundary.

The function returns a pointer to the allocated memory, or \fBNULL\fR if memory allocation fails.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBmemalign_alloc\fR, \fBmemalign_free\fR, and \fBmemalign_alloc_aligned\fR functions to allocate and free memory:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/memalign.h>

int main() {
    void* ptr = memalign_alloc(64, 1024);

    if(ptr != NULL) {
        // Use the allocated memory

        memalign_free(ptr);
    } else {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
    }

    void* aligned_ptr = memalign_alloc_aligned(1024);

    if(aligned_ptr != NULL) {
        // Use the aligned memory

        memalign_free(aligned_ptr);
    } else {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
.nef